---
import PNotes from "../components/PNotes.astro";
---

<p-slide autoplay="true" timing="800">
  <h4 style="margin-block-end: 0.5em" class="center">
    Remember our heavy task? 🤔
  </h4>
  <pre
    class="small-line"
    data-label-file="heavy-task.js">
    <code is:raw class="language-javascript">
  const heavyTask = () => {
    for (let i = 0; i < 10000000000; i++) {
      //do something useful
    }
  };</code>
  </pre>
  <p-fragment timing-start="1200">
    <h4 style="margin-block-start: 0.5em" class="center">
      Cute. <span class="main-sub-gradient">Now meet the real one</span> 🤖
    </h4>
  </p-fragment>
  <PNotes>
    <p>Callback to the opening: the fake for loop was the tutorial version.</p>
    <p>
      In 2026 the heavy task your users will actually hit is AI inference
      running locally in the browser.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h3>🤗 <span class="main-gradient">transformers.js</span></h3>
  <ul class="space mid-dimension" style="--web-day-ul-space: 0.2em">
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      🧠 <span class="main-sub-gradient">Hugging Face models</span> directly in the
      browser — no server
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      🔌 Runs on <span class="main-sub-gradient">ONNX Runtime</span> — WASM (CPU)
      or WebGPU
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="zoom">
      📦 Model downloaded once, then
      <span class="main-sub-gradient"> cached by the browser</span>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      ✈️ Works <span class="main-sub-gradient">offline</span> — no API keys, no per-token
      billing
    </li>
  </ul>
  <PNotes>
    <p>Official Hugging Face library, mirrors the Python transformers API.</p>
    <p>
      v4 shipped in Feb 2026 with a new WebGPU runtime written in C++ with the
      ONNX Runtime team.
    </p>
    <p>
      Models are ONNX conversions, thousands ready on the Hub. Quantized
      sentiment model ≈ 65MB.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h4 style="margin-block-end: 0.5em" class="center">Let&#39;s Code 👨‍💻</h4>
  <pre
    class="small-line"
    data-label-file="sentiment.js">
    <code is:raw class="language-javascript">
  import { pipeline } from '@huggingface/transformers';


  const classifier = await pipeline('sentiment-analysis');</code>
    <p-fragment><code is:raw class="language-javascript">
  const result = await classifier('I love web workers!');</code></p-fragment><p-fragment><code is:raw class="language-javascript">
  // [{ label: 'POSITIVE', score: 0.9998 }]</code>
    </p-fragment>
  </pre>
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      pipeline() does everything: download, cache, tokenization, inference,
      post-processing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Tasks: sentiment, embeddings, translation, Whisper speech-to-text, image
      classification…
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h3>📍 <span class="main-gradient">But where does it run?</span></h3>
  <ul class="space mid-dimension" style="--web-day-ul-space: 0.2em">
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      🧮 <span class="main-sub-gradient">WASM backend</span>: inference runs on
      <b>the thread that calls it</b>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="zoom">
      💀 Call it from the main thread →
      <span class="main-sub-gradient">it IS heavyTask()</span>, for real
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      ⚡ <span class="main-sub-gradient">WebGPU</span> helps, but tokenization &amp;
      post-processing stay on CPU
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      📣 Every official HF demo runs the pipeline
      <span class="main-sub-gradient"> inside a Web Worker</span>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      This is the bridge: not my personal hack, it's the canonical pattern of
      the library.
    </p>
    <p>
      WebGPU global support ~70%, automatic fallback to WASM — so the blocking
      path is the one most users may hit.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide follow-fragments>
  <h4 style="margin-block-end: 0.5em" class="center">Let&#39;s Code 👨‍💻</h4>
  <pre
    class="small-line"
    data-label-file="ai.worker.ts">
    <code is:raw class="language-javascript">
  /// &lt;reference lib="webworker" />
  import { pipeline } from '@huggingface/transformers';

  const classifier = await pipeline(
    'sentiment-analysis',
    'Xenova/distilbert-base-uncased-finetuned-sst-2-english',
    { dtype: 'q8' }
  );</code>
    <p-fragment>
      <code is:raw class="language-javascript">
  addEventListener('message', async ({ data }) => {
    const result = await classifier(data); // 🤖 the REAL heavy task
    postMessage(result);
  });</code>
    </p-fragment>
  </pre>
  <p
    p-fragment
    p-effect="from below"
    class="center mid-dimension"
    style="margin-block-start: 0.5em"
  >
    😏 Same worker, same <code>postMessage</code>, same
    <span class="main-sub-gradient"> resource()</span> pattern as before
  </p>
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      Structurally identical to worker-test.worker.ts from earlier — only
      heavyTask() changed.
    </p>
    <p>
      In Angular: prompt in a signal → resource() → this worker → isLoading()
      while the model crunches. Zero new architecture.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <iframe
    src="/demo/ai-live-demo.html"
    title="AI inference live demo: main thread vs worker"
    style="width: 92%; aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; border: 1px solid #e4e4ee; border-radius: 0.3em; background: #fff; margin-inline: auto; display: block;"
  ></iframe>
  <p-fragment iframe-key="1" p-effect="none"></p-fragment>
  <p-fragment iframe-key="2" p-effect="none"></p-fragment>
  <PNotes>
    <p>Click INSIDE the iframe first, then [2] worker → [1] main.</p>
    <p>
      While main runs, spam the click button — clicks replay all at once when
      the thread frees up.
    </p>
    <p>
      The ball is rAF-driven on purpose: a CSS animation runs on the compositor
      and would keep moving even with a blocked main thread.
    </p>
    <p>Click OUTSIDE the iframe before using arrow keys to advance the deck.</p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h3>🎒 <span class="main-gradient">Good to know</span></h3>
  <ul class="space mid-dimension" style="--web-day-ul-space: 0.2em">
    <li p-fragment p-effect="zoom">
      📥 First load downloads the model —
      <span class="main-sub-gradient">show progress</span>
      (<code>progress_callback</code>) and pre-cache
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      🎚️ <span class="main-sub-gradient">Quantization</span> (<code
        >dtype: 'q8'</code
      >/<code>'q4'</code>) — smaller download, less RAM
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      🚚 Tensors are <code>ArrayBuffer</code>s —
      <span class="main-sub-gradient">transferable objects</span>, remember?
    </li>
  </ul>
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      Pre-cache tip: load the pipeline once on app bootstrap (or a service
      worker) so users never wait on click.
    </p>
    <p>
      The transfer bullet ties back to the postMessage(message, transfer) slide
      — this is where it stops being theoretical.
    </p>
    <p>
      Close the section by pointing at Davide's talk: I show WHERE this stuff
      must run, he shows WHAT LLMs can do with it.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>
